<body>
  <GameNav />
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> > Rotate Rings Guide</nav>

  <main class="seo-section">
    <h1>Rotate Rings Guide: every burst timed, nothing made up</h1>

    <div class="disclaimer">Unofficial fan-made guide. Rotate Rings is a mobile game by Nebula Studio; this site is not affiliated with or endorsed by the developer. Board diagrams are redrawn from video footage and your layout may vary by game version.</div>

    <div class="verify-note">Our walkthroughs are built from footage, not memory: every burst timestamp, score and endgame order is read off real gameplay video frame by frame before it goes on the page. Waves verified at 4 frames per second are marked <b>frame-verified</b>; waves timed at 1 fps are marked <b>★</b> with a ±1s tolerance.</div>

    <h2>What Rotate Rings actually is<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Most descriptions of Rotate Rings get it half right. The board is not a pile of interlocked rings — it is a <b>network of pipe segments</b> in five colors: C-shaped arcs, L-shapes, straight pieces and closed circles, joined end-to-end by silver connectors with a central cross hub where branches meet.</p>
    <p>You rotate the loose segments until each color's open ends meet and the loop closes. A closed loop bursts off the board with a score pop — and here is the part that makes good players: <b>bursts chain</b>. Removing one loop can free the segments that were holding the next one, so a single correct turn can set off a wave that clears a whole cluster. Levels end with the board empty and a leaderboard mini-game called the Acorn Challenge.</p>

    <h2>How to play<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Find the segments that hold nothing.</b> Pieces whose rotation blocks no other segment are safe to turn — turning them first sets up the opening chain.</li>
      <li><b>Work inward, color by color.</b> Match open ends of the same color until the loop closes and bursts.</li>
      <li><b>Watch for chain reactions.</b> When a loop bursts, look at what it released — the next loop is often one turn away.</li>
      <li><b>Closed circles never turn.</b> The full-circle pieces are fixed; every route has to be built around them.</li>
      <li><b>Grab the acorns.</b> Golden acorns ride on specific segments and are collected when their loop bursts — they feed the Acorn Challenge leaderboard after the level.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How our walkthroughs are made<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Each level page is checked against real gameplay footage frame by frame. For a typical level we sample the full video at 1 frame per second to map the burst waves, then re-scan the key moments at 4 frames per second to pin down exact timestamps, colors, scores and the endgame sequence. What you will not find here: invented move orders. If a step could not be confirmed on screen, we mark it as approximate or leave it out — a guide you can trust beats a guide that guesses.</p>

